  "path": "straight cardinal ray from each s facing edge toward the other marker, stopping after half of the background gap; an odd middle line stays background",
  "trajectory": "paint the traversed gap rows/columns with s.color across the full grid span perpendicular to the path",
  "transform": "keep the original marker rectangles and unpainted background cells unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    structure: grid_perp = rect_perp + 2; grid_axial = 2*rect_axial + gap + 2
    perpendicular_dim: 3-6 (rect_perp + 2 margin)
    axial_dim: 14-22 (dominated by gap)
  background: single color, most frequent
  markers:
    count: exactly 2 solid monochromatic rectangles
    colors: two distinct non-bg colors
    size: same for both; rect_axial 1-2, rect_perp 1-4
    placement: margin of exactly 1 on all sides [observed]; aligned sharing full row-span (horizontal) or full col-span (vertical)
    gap: 10-16 bg cells between facing edges along axis [observed, solver requires >=1]
  alignment: horizontal (side-by-side) or vertical (stacked)
  output_rule: |
    Each marker extends toward the other, painting gap//2 rows/cols with its color
    across the FULL perpendicular grid dimension (not just the rect span).
    If gap is odd, the center row/col remains background.

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose alignment (H or V), rect_axial in [1,2], rect_perp in [1,4], gap in [6,16].
    2. Compute grid dims: perp = rect_perp + 2, axial = 2*rect_axial + gap + 2.
    3. Pick bg color and two distinct marker colors.
    4. Build output grid filled with bg; place both rects at margin=1 from edges.
    5. Fill gap//2 rows/cols on each side with respective marker color spanning full perp dimension.
    6. Derive input by erasing the filled gap (set those cells back to bg), keeping only the original rects.
